WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:04.883 --> 00:00:07.693
Hello, hello, hello, and welcome

00:00:07.693 --> 00:00:09.483
to English Learning for Curious

00:00:09.483 --> 00:00:11.413
Minds, by Leonardo English.

00:00:11.803 --> 00:00:14.293
I'm Alastair Budge, and this is part

00:00:14.313 --> 00:00:17.383
two of our three-part series on Ireland:

00:00:17.823 --> 00:00:19.903
nine centuries of struggle between

00:00:19.903 --> 00:00:22.123
an island and its powerful neighbour.

00:00:22.643 --> 00:00:25.533
In part one, we followed Ireland from the

00:00:25.543 --> 00:00:29.363
Norman arrival of 1169 to the Cromwellian

00:00:29.373 --> 00:00:33.003
settlement of the 1650s: how what began

00:00:33.053 --> 00:00:35.743
as a temporary military favour became

00:00:35.743 --> 00:00:38.123
a permanent occupation that stripped

00:00:38.153 --> 00:00:40.453
the Catholic majority of their land.

00:00:40.963 --> 00:00:43.363
In this episode, we move through the

00:00:43.413 --> 00:00:45.163
eighteenth and nineteenth centuries:

00:00:45.533 --> 00:00:47.863
from a failed rebellion that triggered

00:00:47.863 --> 00:00:50.483
an Act of Union, through the catastrophic

00:00:50.493 --> 00:00:52.453
Great Famine, to the brilliant

00:00:52.523 --> 00:00:55.285
political near-miss of the 1880s.

00:00:55.713 --> 00:00:57.923
And next up, in part three,

00:00:58.143 --> 00:00:59.833
we follow Ireland through

00:00:59.843 --> 00:01:01.763
revolution to independence.

00:01:02.243 --> 00:01:03.933
If you haven't listened to part one

00:01:03.933 --> 00:01:06.838
yet, now is the time to press pause and

00:01:06.838 --> 00:01:09.368
catch up on that; this episode follows

00:01:09.378 --> 00:01:11.508
directly on from where we left off.

00:01:12.478 --> 00:01:15.158
OK then, let's get right into it.

00:01:16.248 --> 00:01:18.448
Now, after the Cromwellian

00:01:18.458 --> 00:01:21.448
conquest of the 1650s, Ireland

00:01:21.618 --> 00:01:24.168
was a country defined by division.

00:01:25.368 --> 00:01:27.318
Catholic landowners had been

00:01:27.328 --> 00:01:29.128
stripped of their estates.

00:01:29.628 --> 00:01:32.458
The land was now overwhelmingly in

00:01:32.488 --> 00:01:35.598
Protestant hands: either Anglo-Irish

00:01:35.598 --> 00:01:37.508
families who had been in Ireland

00:01:37.518 --> 00:01:40.568
for generations, or new settlers who

00:01:40.568 --> 00:01:42.788
had arrived with Cromwell's army.

00:01:43.714 --> 00:01:45.464
You might be wondering... what

00:01:45.504 --> 00:01:48.314
exactly was Ireland, politically?

00:01:48.694 --> 00:01:50.194
Was it a colony?

00:01:50.624 --> 00:01:51.964
An independent country?

00:01:52.284 --> 00:01:53.704
Several different countries?

00:01:54.514 --> 00:01:56.854
The answer is "it's complicated".

00:01:58.014 --> 00:02:00.134
Since Henry VIII had declared

00:02:00.134 --> 00:02:02.154
himself King of Ireland in the

00:02:02.164 --> 00:02:05.604
1540s, Ireland had been a separate

00:02:05.604 --> 00:02:08.384
kingdom, not a colony, not fully

00:02:08.424 --> 00:02:11.244
independent, but something in between.

00:02:12.324 --> 00:02:13.754
It shared a monarch with

00:02:13.784 --> 00:02:15.664
England and later with Britain.

00:02:16.344 --> 00:02:17.814
And it had its own

00:02:17.874 --> 00:02:19.724
parliament, based in Dublin.

00:02:20.504 --> 00:02:22.614
But that parliament was not

00:02:22.624 --> 00:02:25.024
representative of most Irish people.

00:02:25.904 --> 00:02:27.984
Catholics, who made up the vast

00:02:27.984 --> 00:02:29.364
majority of the population,

00:02:29.864 --> 00:02:31.594
were largely excluded from it.

00:02:32.524 --> 00:02:34.694
The people who sat in the Irish

00:02:34.714 --> 00:02:37.124
Parliament, who wrote Irish laws,

00:02:37.394 --> 00:02:39.474
and who governed Ireland, they

00:02:39.474 --> 00:02:42.120
were overwhelmingly Protestant.

00:02:42.744 --> 00:02:45.604
The system served their interests,

00:02:45.784 --> 00:02:48.044
which were closely tied to those of

00:02:48.044 --> 00:02:50.774
the English, and later British, state.

00:02:52.204 --> 00:02:54.634
This is the arrangement we need to keep

00:02:54.634 --> 00:02:57.424
in mind as we move through this episode.

00:02:57.854 --> 00:03:00.564
Because everything that follows is, in

00:03:00.564 --> 00:03:03.064
one way or another, a consequence of it.

00:03:04.504 --> 00:03:07.754
The next blow to Catholic Ireland came

00:03:07.794 --> 00:03:09.834
at the end of the seventeenth century,

00:03:10.224 --> 00:03:12.604
through a war that was not, in its

00:03:12.604 --> 00:03:15.114
origin, really about Ireland at all.

00:03:16.104 --> 00:03:20.454
In 1688, the Catholic King James II

00:03:20.454 --> 00:03:22.954
of England was overthrown in what

00:03:22.984 --> 00:03:25.884
historians call the Glorious Revolution.

00:03:27.084 --> 00:03:29.234
His replacement was the Dutch

00:03:29.294 --> 00:03:31.714
Protestant, William of Orange, who

00:03:31.714 --> 00:03:34.274
was invited to take the English throne

00:03:34.644 --> 00:03:36.534
by a group of Protestant nobles.

00:03:37.184 --> 00:03:39.354
It was, in essence, a

00:03:39.354 --> 00:03:41.464
religious and political coup.

00:03:42.784 --> 00:03:45.344
James, however, refused

00:03:45.364 --> 00:03:47.004
to accept his removal.

00:03:47.744 --> 00:03:50.174
He gathered support, and found

00:03:50.184 --> 00:03:52.804
his most enthusiastic backers in

00:03:52.944 --> 00:03:55.684
Ireland, where the Catholic majority

00:03:56.044 --> 00:03:59.054
hoped a Catholic king might restore

00:03:59.174 --> 00:04:00.534
what they'd lost under Cromwell.

00:04:01.504 --> 00:04:04.604
James came to Ireland with French backing.

00:04:05.384 --> 00:04:06.324
William followed.

00:04:07.024 --> 00:04:09.104
The war between them was fought,

00:04:09.484 --> 00:04:12.124
in large part, on Irish soil.

00:04:13.324 --> 00:04:15.374
The decisive moment came at the

00:04:15.384 --> 00:04:18.560
Battle of the Boyne in July 1690,

00:04:18.977 --> 00:04:21.194
a river crossing north of Dublin.

00:04:22.144 --> 00:04:23.684
William's army faced

00:04:23.684 --> 00:04:25.844
James's forces and won.

00:04:26.614 --> 00:04:28.404
James fled to France.

00:04:28.704 --> 00:04:31.504
And Ireland's Catholics, who had backed

00:04:31.544 --> 00:04:34.364
the losing side, faced the consequences.

00:04:35.694 --> 00:04:37.704
Now, the Battle of the Boyne

00:04:38.114 --> 00:04:39.624
has never been forgotten.

00:04:40.574 --> 00:04:43.314
You may have heard of the Orange marches:

00:04:43.664 --> 00:04:46.044
annual parades held in Northern Ireland,

00:04:46.214 --> 00:04:49.134
particularly in July, in which men carry

00:04:49.164 --> 00:04:52.664
banners, beat drums, and walk through city

00:04:52.664 --> 00:04:55.954
streets often watched by tense crowds.

00:04:57.214 --> 00:05:00.174
These marches are organised by the

00:05:00.204 --> 00:05:03.034
Orange Order, a Protestant organisation

00:05:03.444 --> 00:05:07.004
founded in the 1790s, which commemorates

00:05:07.144 --> 00:05:09.764
William of Orange's victory, a victory

00:05:09.994 --> 00:05:13.214
of a Protestant army over a Catholic one.

00:05:14.284 --> 00:05:17.654
So when you see footage of those parades,

00:05:18.004 --> 00:05:19.784
the colour, the noise, and the tension,

00:05:21.214 --> 00:05:24.164
you are watching a 17th-century argument

00:05:24.564 --> 00:05:27.634
playing out on 21st-century streets.

00:05:29.064 --> 00:05:31.304
And following William's victory

00:05:31.514 --> 00:05:33.854
was a comprehensive set of laws

00:05:34.154 --> 00:05:36.984
designed to keep Catholics firmly

00:05:37.014 --> 00:05:39.454
at the bottom of Irish society.

00:05:40.344 --> 00:05:43.364
These are known as the Penal Laws.

00:05:44.274 --> 00:05:46.024
Catholics could not vote.

00:05:46.164 --> 00:05:47.594
They could not sit in Parliament.

00:05:47.874 --> 00:05:49.964
They could not hold public office.

00:05:50.154 --> 00:05:51.724
They could not buy land, or

00:05:51.724 --> 00:05:53.564
inherit it from Protestants.

00:05:53.944 --> 00:05:56.564
Catholic schools were formally forbidden.

00:05:57.884 --> 00:06:00.264
Now, the Penal Laws were

00:06:00.264 --> 00:06:02.274
not enforced uniformly.

00:06:03.540 --> 00:06:05.380
In practice, the life of an

00:06:05.440 --> 00:06:07.600
ordinary Catholic farmer went

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:09.730
on more or less as before.

00:06:10.530 --> 00:06:13.650
But the laws defined the legal position

00:06:13.860 --> 00:06:16.880
of the Catholic majority as second-class

00:06:16.930 --> 00:06:19.100
subjects in their own country.

00:06:20.040 --> 00:06:22.230
The people who owned the land,

00:06:22.470 --> 00:06:24.360
governed the country, and made the

00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:27.210
laws were overwhelmingly Protestant.

00:06:27.700 --> 00:06:30.340
The people who worked the land, paid the

00:06:30.340 --> 00:06:33.370
rents, and had no say in how Ireland was

00:06:33.370 --> 00:06:36.490
run: they were the Catholic majority.

00:06:37.350 --> 00:06:39.460
That was the Ireland of the

00:06:39.540 --> 00:06:40.840
early eighteenth century.

00:06:41.190 --> 00:06:43.150
And it is the starting point

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:44.630
for everything that follows.

00:06:45.800 --> 00:06:49.280
Now, in 1789, an event on the

00:06:49.400 --> 00:06:50.940
other side of the English Channel

00:06:51.330 --> 00:06:53.520
sent a shockwave through Europe.

00:06:54.470 --> 00:06:57.050
The French Revolution swept away

00:06:57.050 --> 00:06:59.220
the French monarchy, proclaimed the

00:06:59.220 --> 00:07:01.300
rights of man, and declared that

00:07:01.300 --> 00:07:04.090
the people, not kings, not inherited

00:07:04.090 --> 00:07:06.699
privilege, they should be sovereign.

00:07:07.706 --> 00:07:10.636
Perhaps unsurprisingly, given everything

00:07:10.636 --> 00:07:13.006
you've heard so far, in Ireland,

00:07:13.326 --> 00:07:15.926
that message found willing ears.

00:07:17.356 --> 00:07:19.616
One man who felt it particularly

00:07:19.616 --> 00:07:21.746
strongly was a lawyer from

00:07:21.756 --> 00:07:24.546
Dublin named Theobald Wolfe Tone.

00:07:25.666 --> 00:07:29.996
Now, Wolfe Tone was a Protestant, but he

00:07:30.006 --> 00:07:32.786
had a vision of Ireland in which Catholics

00:07:32.996 --> 00:07:36.656
and Protestants alike would be united as

00:07:36.736 --> 00:07:40.616
Irish citizens, free from British control.

00:07:41.376 --> 00:07:44.196
He believed that Britain deliberately

00:07:44.426 --> 00:07:47.086
kept these groups divided, because a

00:07:47.086 --> 00:07:50.056
divided Ireland was easier to dominate.

00:07:51.256 --> 00:07:53.296
He co-founded the Society

00:07:53.296 --> 00:07:56.433
of United Irishmen in 1791.

00:07:57.226 --> 00:08:01.416
And by 1798, the society was

00:08:01.476 --> 00:08:03.586
planning an armed rebellion,

00:08:03.916 --> 00:08:06.026
with French military support.

00:08:07.476 --> 00:08:08.856
The rebellion would

00:08:08.876 --> 00:08:10.626
prove to be a disaster.

00:08:12.146 --> 00:08:14.406
It broke out in pockets across Ireland

00:08:14.676 --> 00:08:17.696
and was crushed separately in each one.

00:08:18.336 --> 00:08:20.646
The government's response was brutal.

00:08:21.486 --> 00:08:24.456
In the words of a prominent Anglo-Irish

00:08:24.456 --> 00:08:26.486
historian, "In the space of a few

00:08:26.486 --> 00:08:29.766
weeks, 30,000 – peasants armed with

00:08:29.836 --> 00:08:32.326
pikes and pitchforks, defenceless

00:08:32.346 --> 00:08:34.536
women and children – were cut down,

00:08:34.886 --> 00:08:38.046
shot, or blown like chaff as they

00:08:38.106 --> 00:08:40.246
charged up to the mouth of the cannon."

00:08:41.806 --> 00:08:44.266
Wolfe Tone, the leader, was captured

00:08:44.286 --> 00:08:46.906
at sea, sentenced to death, and

00:08:47.086 --> 00:08:49.456
died in prison before his execution,

00:08:49.966 --> 00:08:51.866
apparently by his own hand.

00:08:52.746 --> 00:08:55.266
He was thirty-five, and he became the

00:08:55.276 --> 00:08:58.406
founding martyr of Irish republicanism.

00:09:00.006 --> 00:09:02.606
And if Wolfe Tone had hoped that

00:09:02.616 --> 00:09:05.236
his death, and the death of his

00:09:05.236 --> 00:09:06.996
fellow rebels, would not be in

00:09:06.996 --> 00:09:09.386
vain, and would at least spark a

00:09:09.386 --> 00:09:11.836
discussion about Irish independence,

00:09:12.256 --> 00:09:14.396
he would have been much mistaken.

00:09:15.606 --> 00:09:17.686
The British Prime Minister, William

00:09:17.686 --> 00:09:20.966
Pitt, had long wanted to dissolve the

00:09:20.966 --> 00:09:23.346
Irish Parliament and bring Ireland

00:09:23.526 --> 00:09:26.446
fully under direct British rule.

00:09:27.426 --> 00:09:29.666
The Irish Parliament, that

00:09:29.776 --> 00:09:31.626
Protestant-dominated body in Dublin,

00:09:32.086 --> 00:09:33.786
had been a source of frustration for

00:09:33.786 --> 00:09:35.946
the British government for decades.

00:09:36.366 --> 00:09:38.206
It was unpredictable.

00:09:38.716 --> 00:09:40.326
It could be used against

00:09:40.396 --> 00:09:41.406
British interests.

00:09:42.416 --> 00:09:45.846
And the 1798 rebellion gave Pitt

00:09:45.996 --> 00:09:47.670
the excuse he was looking for.

00:09:48.246 --> 00:09:51.496
It proved, so he said, that a separate

00:09:51.596 --> 00:09:54.496
Irish parliament was simply too dangerous.

00:09:55.716 --> 00:10:00.056
So, in 1800, the Act of Union was passed.

00:10:01.096 --> 00:10:05.226
From the first of January 1801, Ireland

00:10:05.406 --> 00:10:07.396
was no longer a separate kingdom.

00:10:08.126 --> 00:10:10.506
It was formally part of a new

00:10:10.556 --> 00:10:12.746
entity: the United Kingdom

00:10:12.936 --> 00:10:15.106
of Great Britain and Ireland.

00:10:16.136 --> 00:10:18.446
England and Scotland had united

00:10:18.506 --> 00:10:20.436
nearly a century earlier, in

00:10:20.656 --> 00:10:23.196
1707, to form Great Britain.

00:10:24.046 --> 00:10:26.756
Now Great Britain and Ireland were

00:10:26.756 --> 00:10:29.226
being joined together, on paper,

00:10:29.566 --> 00:10:31.986
as equal partners in one nation.

00:10:33.576 --> 00:10:35.896
In practice, it meant that Ireland's

00:10:35.896 --> 00:10:38.316
affairs were decided by a parliament

00:10:38.336 --> 00:10:41.202
in Westminster, London, in which Irish

00:10:41.242 --> 00:10:45.082
MPs were a permanent minority, and in

00:10:45.082 --> 00:10:47.242
which Ireland's commercial interests

00:10:47.432 --> 00:10:49.672
were subordinate to British ones.

00:10:50.732 --> 00:10:53.312
To push this through, though, Pitt

00:10:53.612 --> 00:10:55.702
had to offer something in return.

00:10:56.362 --> 00:10:59.022
He privately assured Catholic leaders that

00:10:59.032 --> 00:11:01.892
the Union would be followed by Catholic

00:11:01.982 --> 00:11:04.672
Emancipation, meaning that Catholics

00:11:04.832 --> 00:11:07.102
would finally be allowed to sit in

00:11:07.132 --> 00:11:10.152
Parliament and hold most public offices.

00:11:11.562 --> 00:11:14.622
King George III, however, refused.

00:11:15.462 --> 00:11:17.662
He believed that allowing Catholics

00:11:17.702 --> 00:11:20.762
into Parliament would violate the oath

00:11:20.772 --> 00:11:23.122
he had sworn at his coronation: an

00:11:23.132 --> 00:11:25.482
oath to defend the Protestant faith.

00:11:26.472 --> 00:11:28.592
William Pitt resigned in protest.

00:11:28.852 --> 00:11:31.052
And Catholic Emancipation would not

00:11:31.082 --> 00:11:33.442
come for another twenty-eight years.

00:11:34.252 --> 00:11:36.922
For Ireland, the Union had promised

00:11:36.942 --> 00:11:39.672
much and delivered very little.

00:11:41.035 --> 00:11:43.975
The man who finally forced the issue was

00:11:43.985 --> 00:11:46.285
unlike anyone who had come before him.

00:11:47.345 --> 00:11:49.405
Daniel O'Connell was a Catholic

00:11:49.425 --> 00:11:51.135
lawyer from County Kerry.

00:11:51.695 --> 00:11:53.375
He was, by any measure,

00:11:53.605 --> 00:11:54.865
a remarkable figure.

00:11:55.665 --> 00:11:57.145
He had a gift for public

00:11:57.145 --> 00:11:59.535
speaking, and was compared to the

00:11:59.545 --> 00:12:01.995
great orators of ancient Rome.

00:12:02.875 --> 00:12:05.503
And he had a talent for organising

00:12:05.575 --> 00:12:06.985
ordinary people that was

00:12:07.025 --> 00:12:09.495
entirely new in Irish politics.

00:12:10.555 --> 00:12:12.895
He had seen what physical force had

00:12:12.895 --> 00:12:16.745
brought in 1798, mass death and the

00:12:16.755 --> 00:12:18.755
loss of Ireland's parliament, and

00:12:19.005 --> 00:12:21.895
he had drawn a firm conclusion: the

00:12:21.925 --> 00:12:24.605
only way forward was constitutional.

00:12:25.275 --> 00:12:25.995
Organise.

00:12:26.465 --> 00:12:26.995
Petition.

00:12:27.475 --> 00:12:28.035
Elect.

00:12:28.655 --> 00:12:29.245
Legislate.

00:12:30.355 --> 00:12:33.705
From 1823, he led something called the

00:12:33.785 --> 00:12:36.485
Catholic Association, collecting small

00:12:36.495 --> 00:12:38.685
weekly subscriptions from ordinary

00:12:38.745 --> 00:12:40.645
Irish Catholics across the country.

00:12:41.585 --> 00:12:44.455
The amounts were tiny, a penny or two

00:12:44.455 --> 00:12:47.485
a week, but the scale was immense.

00:12:48.235 --> 00:12:50.375
Within a few years, it was the

00:12:50.375 --> 00:12:52.855
first genuinely mass political

00:12:52.855 --> 00:12:54.615
movement in Irish history.

00:12:55.915 --> 00:12:58.925
In 1828, O'Connell stood for

00:12:58.965 --> 00:13:00.615
election in County Clare.

00:13:01.265 --> 00:13:02.355
He won easily.

00:13:03.335 --> 00:13:05.315
But here was the problem.

00:13:06.055 --> 00:13:08.115
Under laws dating from the late

00:13:08.145 --> 00:13:10.785
seventeenth century, anyone wishing

00:13:10.785 --> 00:13:13.935
to sit in Parliament had to swear a

00:13:13.965 --> 00:13:17.055
series of oaths: oaths that acknowledged

00:13:17.085 --> 00:13:19.265
the Protestant monarch as the head

00:13:19.275 --> 00:13:22.225
of the Church, oaths that no Catholic

00:13:22.355 --> 00:13:24.135
could swear in good conscience.

00:13:25.215 --> 00:13:28.095
So O’Connell’s victory exposed

00:13:28.105 --> 00:13:30.865
something fundamental: Catholics

00:13:30.895 --> 00:13:33.135
could be elected, but they were

00:13:33.135 --> 00:13:35.855
still effectively barred from fully

00:13:35.885 --> 00:13:37.885
taking part in the political system.

00:13:39.175 --> 00:13:41.035
The British government found

00:13:41.035 --> 00:13:42.925
itself facing a crisis.

00:13:43.315 --> 00:13:45.755
The Duke of Wellington — the general

00:13:45.895 --> 00:13:47.885
who had defeated Napoleon at Waterloo,

00:13:48.065 --> 00:13:49.775
and who was now serving as Prime

00:13:49.775 --> 00:13:52.225
Minister — he had no particular

00:13:52.225 --> 00:13:54.455
sympathy for Irish Catholics.

00:13:54.975 --> 00:13:57.630
But he was a pragmatist.

00:13:58.435 --> 00:14:00.075
What O'Connell's election had

00:14:00.085 --> 00:14:02.505
demonstrated was that the Catholic

00:14:02.505 --> 00:14:04.875
population of Ireland was organised,

00:14:05.275 --> 00:14:07.985
disciplined, and not going to stop.

00:14:09.015 --> 00:14:11.225
You could refuse to seat O'Connell.

00:14:11.535 --> 00:14:13.625
But you could not keep every

00:14:13.635 --> 00:14:15.605
future Catholic candidate out

00:14:15.605 --> 00:14:16.935
of Parliament indefinitely.

00:14:17.595 --> 00:14:20.125
And if you tried to hold the line

00:14:20.125 --> 00:14:23.065
by force, you risked open rebellion

00:14:23.065 --> 00:14:25.505
in Ireland, which, after everything

00:14:25.515 --> 00:14:27.695
that had already happened, was not a

00:14:27.695 --> 00:14:29.775
risk Wellington was prepared to take.

00:14:30.555 --> 00:14:34.315
So, in 1829, the law changed.

00:14:34.715 --> 00:14:37.305
Catholics could now sit in Parliament

00:14:37.545 --> 00:14:39.615
and hold most public offices.

00:14:40.175 --> 00:14:42.755
This was Catholic Emancipation.

00:14:44.005 --> 00:14:46.235
O'Connell crossed to Westminster

00:14:46.475 --> 00:14:48.885
and became the first Catholic MP

00:14:49.255 --> 00:14:51.375
since the Penal Laws had effectively

00:14:51.385 --> 00:14:53.085
barred Catholics from holding

00:14:53.085 --> 00:14:56.175
office, and in Ireland, he became

00:14:56.175 --> 00:14:58.465
something close to a national hero.

00:14:59.275 --> 00:15:01.415
He earned the title by which history

00:15:01.425 --> 00:15:03.115
remembers him: the Liberator.

00:15:04.295 --> 00:15:06.275
But he wanted more.

00:15:07.295 --> 00:15:10.055
From the 1830s onward, he campaigned

00:15:10.265 --> 00:15:13.115
for the Repeal of the Act of Union: for

00:15:13.115 --> 00:15:15.605
Ireland to have its own parliament again.

00:15:16.325 --> 00:15:18.635
He held vast outdoor meetings.

00:15:19.065 --> 00:15:23.205
At Tara in 1843, the ancient seat of the

00:15:23.205 --> 00:15:26.465
High Kings, he drew a crowd estimated

00:15:26.645 --> 00:15:29.155
at three-quarters of a million people.

00:15:30.595 --> 00:15:33.005
The government, which was alarmed

00:15:33.035 --> 00:15:34.555
by the movement O'Connell was

00:15:34.555 --> 00:15:37.365
building, decided it had had enough.

00:15:37.935 --> 00:15:39.775
It banned his next planned

00:15:39.805 --> 00:15:42.555
meeting, and O'Connell called it

00:15:42.555 --> 00:15:44.845
off rather than risk violence.

00:15:45.505 --> 00:15:47.742
He was arrested, tried,

00:15:47.762 --> 00:15:49.172
and briefly imprisoned.

00:15:50.068 --> 00:15:52.078
His movement began to fracture.

00:15:52.858 --> 00:15:55.678
The younger, more radical members of

00:15:55.678 --> 00:15:57.898
the nationalist movement felt he was

00:15:58.068 --> 00:16:00.678
too cautious, too willing to compromise.

00:16:01.918 --> 00:16:04.628
His health declined, his Repeal

00:16:04.628 --> 00:16:08.458
campaign lost momentum, and in 1847

00:16:08.678 --> 00:16:11.578
he died in Genoa, on his way to Rome.

00:16:13.188 --> 00:16:17.328
And 1847, the year of his death, was

00:16:17.328 --> 00:16:19.668
the worst single year of a catastrophe

00:16:19.998 --> 00:16:21.778
that had already been devastating

00:16:21.778 --> 00:16:25.618
Ireland since 1845: the Great Famine.

00:16:27.068 --> 00:16:29.688
Now, to understand the Great Famine,

00:16:29.998 --> 00:16:32.648
we first need to understand what life

00:16:32.678 --> 00:16:34.958
looked like for most Irish people

00:16:35.138 --> 00:16:36.698
in the early nineteenth century.

00:16:37.548 --> 00:16:39.778
The majority of Ireland's population,

00:16:40.158 --> 00:16:42.718
which was around eight million by

00:16:42.718 --> 00:16:45.548
the 1840s, were tenant farmers.

00:16:46.228 --> 00:16:48.558
They rented small plots of land

00:16:48.618 --> 00:16:50.984
from landlords, most of whom were

00:16:51.052 --> 00:16:53.998
Protestant and many of whom lived

00:16:54.008 --> 00:16:55.778
in England, collecting their

00:16:55.778 --> 00:16:57.988
rents from a distance without ever

00:16:57.998 --> 00:17:00.058
setting foot on their own estates.

00:17:01.388 --> 00:17:05.098
These tenant farmers grew grain, raised

00:17:05.138 --> 00:17:08.058
cattle, and produced butter and pork.

00:17:08.748 --> 00:17:12.788
But almost all of it was exported, grown

00:17:12.788 --> 00:17:15.778
on commercial terms for landlords whose

00:17:15.808 --> 00:17:18.078
income came from the English market.

00:17:19.068 --> 00:17:20.928
The tenant farmers could not

00:17:20.938 --> 00:17:22.818
afford to eat what they grew.

00:17:23.758 --> 00:17:25.478
What they could grow for themselves,

00:17:25.968 --> 00:17:28.738
cheaply, efficiently, and even on

00:17:28.738 --> 00:17:31.038
a very small plot of land, was a

00:17:31.038 --> 00:17:33.828
crop not native to Ireland, but

00:17:33.828 --> 00:17:36.328
to South America: the potato.

00:17:37.448 --> 00:17:40.168
Over the previous century, the potato

00:17:40.368 --> 00:17:42.698
had transformed Irish agriculture.

00:17:43.338 --> 00:17:45.668
A single acre, so less than

00:17:45.668 --> 00:17:47.618
half a hectare, this could

00:17:47.618 --> 00:17:50.208
feed a large family for a year.

00:17:51.518 --> 00:17:54.608
And for the rural majority of Ireland,

00:17:55.218 --> 00:17:57.528
it had become not a supplement to

00:17:57.528 --> 00:18:00.318
their diet, but the centre of it.

00:18:00.828 --> 00:18:02.408
And not out of preference,

00:18:02.758 --> 00:18:03.828
but out of necessity.

00:18:04.778 --> 00:18:08.086
And in the autumn of 1845,

00:18:08.368 --> 00:18:10.588
a fungal disease arrived in

00:18:10.588 --> 00:18:12.338
Ireland from North America.

00:18:12.988 --> 00:18:14.668
It attacked potato plants,

00:18:14.918 --> 00:18:16.498
rotting the crop in the ground

00:18:16.778 --> 00:18:18.578
and turning the leaves black.

00:18:19.588 --> 00:18:21.908
Within weeks, a large part of

00:18:21.908 --> 00:18:24.298
Ireland's potato harvest was destroyed.

00:18:25.188 --> 00:18:27.628
The blight returned in 1846.

00:18:27.628 --> 00:18:30.228
And again in 1847.

00:18:31.118 --> 00:18:32.628
People starved.

00:18:33.368 --> 00:18:34.428
Families were found

00:18:34.438 --> 00:18:35.638
dead in their cottages.

00:18:36.108 --> 00:18:38.948
People were reduced to eating grass,

00:18:39.261 --> 00:18:41.928
boiled nettles, gnawing on bark,

00:18:42.038 --> 00:18:44.548
doing anything they could to survive.

00:18:45.378 --> 00:18:47.718
Hundreds of thousands did

00:18:47.718 --> 00:18:50.628
not, dead by starvation or the

00:18:50.628 --> 00:18:52.438
multiple diseases that followed.

00:18:53.687 --> 00:18:56.338
And throughout the worst years of the

00:18:56.348 --> 00:18:59.848
Famine, food continued to leave Ireland.

00:19:00.568 --> 00:19:04.648
Grain, cattle, butter, pork: all exported

00:19:04.648 --> 00:19:06.948
to England, because the commercial

00:19:06.948 --> 00:19:09.038
contracts that governed them did

00:19:09.038 --> 00:19:10.588
not stop because people were dying.

00:19:11.448 --> 00:19:14.078
Armed guards escorted food shipments

00:19:14.078 --> 00:19:16.338
to the ports as starving people

00:19:16.528 --> 00:19:18.218
watched from the roadsides.

00:19:19.258 --> 00:19:22.298
That image lodged in the Irish memory.

00:19:22.888 --> 00:19:26.198
And for many, it has never quite left.

00:19:27.708 --> 00:19:29.428
The British government's response

00:19:29.728 --> 00:19:31.358
is one of the most debated

00:19:31.358 --> 00:19:33.138
questions in Irish history.

00:19:34.378 --> 00:19:37.648
Some measures were taken: maize was

00:19:37.648 --> 00:19:39.668
bought and distributed to Ireland;

00:19:39.998 --> 00:19:42.088
public works schemes were set up

00:19:42.108 --> 00:19:44.018
to give people wages to buy food.

00:19:44.678 --> 00:19:47.278
But the dominant economic thinking of

00:19:47.278 --> 00:19:50.218
the time held that governments should not

00:19:50.298 --> 00:19:53.594
intervene in markets: that trade should

00:19:53.594 --> 00:19:56.784
be left alone, that private charity rather

00:19:56.784 --> 00:19:59.014
than the state should address suffering.

00:20:00.204 --> 00:20:02.164
And so the response was

00:20:02.204 --> 00:20:03.764
too slow and too small.

00:20:05.454 --> 00:20:08.194
And some officials had more

00:20:08.194 --> 00:20:09.730
radical views towards it all.

00:20:10.794 --> 00:20:13.574
One Treasury official who oversaw the

00:20:13.594 --> 00:20:16.614
relief operation wrote that the Famine was

00:20:16.614 --> 00:20:19.644
a judgement of God, and that it was not

00:20:19.644 --> 00:20:22.454
for man to interfere with God's purposes.

00:20:23.784 --> 00:20:26.334
Public works were wound down just

00:20:26.334 --> 00:20:28.274
as conditions reached their worst.

00:20:28.824 --> 00:20:30.704
Soup kitchens were opened too

00:20:30.704 --> 00:20:32.844
late and closed too early.

00:20:33.544 --> 00:20:35.914
Landlords were made responsible for

00:20:35.944 --> 00:20:38.384
providing poor relief, which gave

00:20:38.384 --> 00:20:41.134
them every incentive to evict starving

00:20:41.134 --> 00:20:43.184
tenants rather than support them.

00:20:44.614 --> 00:20:47.204
By the time the Famine ended, around

00:20:47.543 --> 00:20:51.724
1852, an estimated one million people

00:20:51.934 --> 00:20:54.854
had died of starvation and disease.

00:20:55.394 --> 00:20:58.474
More than two million had emigrated — on

00:20:58.474 --> 00:21:01.504
ships so overcrowded and unsanitary

00:21:01.844 --> 00:21:04.524
that they became known as coffin ships.

00:21:05.294 --> 00:21:07.784
Thousands more died on the crossing.

00:21:09.494 --> 00:21:12.154
Ireland's population fell from around

00:21:12.224 --> 00:21:15.084
eight and a half million in 1845

00:21:15.414 --> 00:21:18.174
to six and a half million by 1851.

00:21:19.094 --> 00:21:21.634
By 1901, it was barely

00:21:21.654 --> 00:21:23.104
four and a half million.

00:21:24.313 --> 00:21:27.084
Emigration, which the Famine had

00:21:27.084 --> 00:21:29.164
turned from a trickle into a flood,

00:21:29.694 --> 00:21:31.624
did not stop when the blight ended.

00:21:32.624 --> 00:21:35.054
And it continued for generations.

00:21:36.044 --> 00:21:38.914
Those who left carried Ireland with them.

00:21:39.474 --> 00:21:42.424
They built communities in New York,

00:21:42.494 --> 00:21:45.684
Boston, Chicago, Melbourne, and Liverpool.

00:21:46.244 --> 00:21:49.604
They kept their identity, remembered where

00:21:49.604 --> 00:21:52.294
they came from, and passed the story of

00:21:52.294 --> 00:21:54.994
the Famine down through their families.

00:21:55.714 --> 00:21:58.314
The great-grandchildren of Famine

00:21:58.364 --> 00:22:00.644
emigrants would go on to become

00:22:00.964 --> 00:22:04.454
senators, mayors, generals — and

00:22:04.694 --> 00:22:07.834
in 1960, one of them with the Irish

00:22:07.864 --> 00:22:09.774
surname "Kennedy" would become

00:22:09.794 --> 00:22:11.664
President of the United States.

00:22:13.344 --> 00:22:16.634
And the anger the Famine created was not

00:22:16.644 --> 00:22:19.734
simply anger at nature, or at bad luck.

00:22:20.144 --> 00:22:24.114
It was anger at the system: at landlords

00:22:24.324 --> 00:22:26.204
collecting rents from people who were

00:22:26.214 --> 00:22:29.254
dying; at a government that allowed food

00:22:29.304 --> 00:22:31.684
to leave the country while its people

00:22:31.694 --> 00:22:34.804
starved; at a union that had promised

00:22:34.844 --> 00:22:37.124
to bring Ireland into the family of

00:22:37.124 --> 00:22:39.934
the United Kingdom, and had instead

00:22:40.244 --> 00:22:43.014
presided over the worst humanitarian

00:22:43.014 --> 00:22:45.394
catastrophe in nineteenth-century Europe.

00:22:46.334 --> 00:22:47.754
The Famine did not

00:22:47.814 --> 00:22:49.804
create Irish nationalism.

00:22:50.184 --> 00:22:53.074
But it gave it a fury and a depth

00:22:53.404 --> 00:22:54.924
that would never quite go away.

00:22:56.414 --> 00:22:59.694
And it was out of this fury, and out

00:22:59.694 --> 00:23:02.164
of the Irish communities now scattered

00:23:02.184 --> 00:23:04.924
across America and beyond, that a

00:23:04.924 --> 00:23:07.174
new kind of organisation emerged.

00:23:08.514 --> 00:23:11.704
In 1858, the Irish Republican

00:23:11.714 --> 00:23:12.884
Brotherhood was founded,

00:23:13.266 --> 00:23:15.984
simultaneously in Dublin and New York.

00:23:17.164 --> 00:23:19.454
In America, it became known as

00:23:19.454 --> 00:23:21.764
the Fenians, a name taken from

00:23:21.764 --> 00:23:24.314
the Fianna, the legendary warrior

00:23:24.314 --> 00:23:26.214
band of ancient Irish myth.

00:23:27.794 --> 00:23:30.114
Their position was stark:

00:23:31.034 --> 00:23:33.434
constitutional politics had failed.

00:23:33.944 --> 00:23:35.834
The British Parliament would never

00:23:35.844 --> 00:23:37.984
willingly give Ireland independence.

00:23:38.604 --> 00:23:40.684
Only force would work.

00:23:42.194 --> 00:23:45.394
The Fenian Rising of 1867 was

00:23:45.394 --> 00:23:47.324
the test of that conviction.

00:23:48.334 --> 00:23:51.424
It was poorly coordinated, as the British

00:23:51.424 --> 00:23:53.324
government had informers inside the

00:23:53.324 --> 00:23:56.044
movement, and it was put down quickly.

00:23:57.274 --> 00:23:59.964
But the organisation survived.

00:24:00.434 --> 00:24:02.604
It went underground, recruited

00:24:02.604 --> 00:24:04.264
carefully, and waited.

00:24:05.434 --> 00:24:07.584
Within the broader nationalist movement,

00:24:07.979 --> 00:24:09.960
it kept alive the conviction that

00:24:09.960 --> 00:24:12.330
when the right moment came, it would

00:24:12.340 --> 00:24:14.706
have to be seized, not asked for.

00:24:15.961 --> 00:24:18.235
But not everyone had

00:24:18.245 --> 00:24:19.465
given up on Parliament.

00:24:20.355 --> 00:24:22.415
If there was a man who could prove

00:24:22.415 --> 00:24:24.115
that conviction wrong — who could

00:24:24.265 --> 00:24:26.615
win Irish self-government through

00:24:26.665 --> 00:24:28.775
constitutional means alone — it

00:24:28.775 --> 00:24:30.775
was Charles Stewart Parnell.

00:24:31.915 --> 00:24:34.815
Parnell came from a Protestant landowning

00:24:34.815 --> 00:24:38.231
family in County Wicklow, a county on

00:24:38.231 --> 00:24:40.481
Ireland's east coast just south of Dublin.

00:24:41.531 --> 00:24:43.571
He was not the obvious leader

00:24:43.851 --> 00:24:45.701
of an Irish Catholic nationalist

00:24:45.701 --> 00:24:48.061
movement: he was reserved,

00:24:48.511 --> 00:24:51.081
aristocratic, not particularly warm.

00:24:51.961 --> 00:24:54.271
But he was ice-cold under

00:24:54.271 --> 00:24:56.791
pressure, and he became the most

00:24:56.801 --> 00:24:58.941
formidable Irish parliamentary

00:24:58.971 --> 00:25:01.481
operator Britain had ever faced.

00:25:02.971 --> 00:25:05.961
His first campaign was for land reform.

00:25:07.111 --> 00:25:10.061
Ireland's land system was still, in

00:25:10.061 --> 00:25:12.711
its essentials, the one the plantations

00:25:12.721 --> 00:25:15.281
had created: Protestant landlords,

00:25:15.541 --> 00:25:18.741
Catholic tenants, rents set at whatever

00:25:18.771 --> 00:25:21.201
the landlord chose, with no legal

00:25:21.201 --> 00:25:23.471
protection for tenants who could not pay.

00:25:24.701 --> 00:25:27.751
In the late 1870s, a combination of

00:25:27.981 --> 00:25:30.871
agricultural depression and bad harvests

00:25:31.121 --> 00:25:33.771
pushed tenant farmers toward ruin.

00:25:35.621 --> 00:25:36.981
Parnell's Land League

00:25:37.251 --> 00:25:38.711
organised resistance.

00:25:39.301 --> 00:25:42.201
And one of its tactics produced

00:25:42.221 --> 00:25:44.291
a word now used in almost

00:25:44.321 --> 00:25:45.801
every language in the world.

00:25:47.421 --> 00:25:50.601
Captain Charles Boycott was an English

00:25:50.641 --> 00:25:54.401
land agent in County Mayo; land agents

00:25:54.601 --> 00:25:56.641
were the people who managed estates

00:25:56.791 --> 00:25:59.101
on behalf of landlords, collecting

00:25:59.101 --> 00:26:01.191
rents and enforcing evictions.

00:26:02.961 --> 00:26:05.601
When Boycott tried to evict

00:26:05.721 --> 00:26:07.951
tenants, the local community

00:26:08.221 --> 00:26:11.081
refused to deal with him in any way.

00:26:11.661 --> 00:26:13.311
No one would harvest his

00:26:13.311 --> 00:26:15.341
crops, serve in his house, sell

00:26:15.371 --> 00:26:17.101
him goods, or speak to him.

00:26:17.811 --> 00:26:20.181
He became so isolated that he

00:26:20.181 --> 00:26:22.671
eventually had to import workers

00:26:22.891 --> 00:26:25.201
under military escort just to

00:26:25.201 --> 00:26:27.871
bring in his harvest, at a cost far

00:26:27.871 --> 00:26:30.011
greater than the harvest was worth.

00:26:31.431 --> 00:26:33.961
His surname became a verb.

00:26:34.431 --> 00:26:37.481
To boycott someone: to refuse

00:26:37.481 --> 00:26:39.351
to deal with them as a form of

00:26:39.351 --> 00:26:41.321
social and economic pressure.

00:26:43.011 --> 00:26:44.671
Land reform eventually

00:26:44.671 --> 00:26:46.281
came through legislation.

00:26:46.941 --> 00:26:49.731
But Parnell's larger goal was

00:26:50.041 --> 00:26:53.091
Home Rule: an Irish parliament for

00:26:53.091 --> 00:26:55.601
domestic affairs, while Ireland

00:26:55.721 --> 00:26:57.541
remained within the United Kingdom.

00:26:58.691 --> 00:27:01.231
And it is worth being clear about what

00:27:01.231 --> 00:27:04.261
that actually meant, because it was not

00:27:04.271 --> 00:27:07.332
the same as what Ireland had lost in 1800.

00:27:08.171 --> 00:27:10.791
The old Irish Parliament, abolished by

00:27:10.791 --> 00:27:13.851
Pitt, had been almost entirely Protestant.

00:27:14.401 --> 00:27:16.463
Catholics could not sit in it.

00:27:16.771 --> 00:27:18.811
It represented the landlord

00:27:18.831 --> 00:27:20.951
class, not the Irish people.

00:27:22.181 --> 00:27:24.171
Home Rule would be different.

00:27:24.591 --> 00:27:27.266
A new parliament, representing all of

00:27:27.266 --> 00:27:29.941
Ireland's people, Catholic and Protestant

00:27:29.971 --> 00:27:32.361
alike, handling domestic matters:

00:27:32.621 --> 00:27:35.751
taxation, education and land policy.

00:27:36.751 --> 00:27:38.791
Westminster would keep control

00:27:38.791 --> 00:27:40.998
of defence and foreign affairs.

00:27:41.191 --> 00:27:43.311
It was not full independence.

00:27:43.781 --> 00:27:46.631
But for the Catholic majority, who had

00:27:46.631 --> 00:27:49.231
never had a parliament that spoke for

00:27:49.231 --> 00:27:51.861
them, it was something genuinely new.

00:27:52.991 --> 00:27:56.271
And by the 1880s, the pressure for

00:27:56.271 --> 00:27:58.661
it had become impossible to ignore.

00:27:59.668 --> 00:28:02.381
Parnell had turned Irish nationalism

00:28:02.671 --> 00:28:04.871
into a powerful parliamentary force

00:28:04.871 --> 00:28:07.481
at Westminster, and the Liberal Prime

00:28:07.481 --> 00:28:10.231
Minister William Gladstone had come to

00:28:10.231 --> 00:28:12.641
believe that the Union was not working.

00:28:13.621 --> 00:28:16.901
His answer was Home Rule: limited

00:28:16.911 --> 00:28:18.811
self-government for Ireland, while

00:28:18.811 --> 00:28:20.951
keeping it inside the United Kingdom.

00:28:21.661 --> 00:28:24.501
So in 1886, Gladstone

00:28:24.701 --> 00:28:26.781
introduced a Home Rule Bill.

00:28:27.321 --> 00:28:29.921
It was defeated in the House of Commons.

00:28:30.781 --> 00:28:33.451
Then Parnell's personal life

00:28:33.591 --> 00:28:35.441
destroyed his political career.

00:28:36.381 --> 00:28:39.961
His downfall came in 1890, when a

00:28:39.961 --> 00:28:41.951
divorce case revealed that he had

00:28:41.951 --> 00:28:43.871
been having an affair with the wife

00:28:43.901 --> 00:28:46.611
of one of his own MPs, an affair

00:28:46.851 --> 00:28:48.741
that had been going on for a decade.

00:28:49.461 --> 00:28:51.961
The Catholic Church turned against him.

00:28:52.441 --> 00:28:54.901
Gladstone withdrew his party's support.

00:28:55.350 --> 00:28:57.540
The Irish Parliamentary Party split.

00:28:58.590 --> 00:29:01.280
Parnell fought on, but he was

00:29:01.280 --> 00:29:03.990
exhausted and ill, and died shortly

00:29:03.990 --> 00:29:06.370
after, aged only forty-five.

00:29:07.740 --> 00:29:09.830
He did not live to see what followed.

00:29:10.280 --> 00:29:12.730
A second Home Rule Bill was passed

00:29:12.730 --> 00:29:15.740
by the Commons in 1893, only to

00:29:15.750 --> 00:29:17.720
be vetoed by the House of Lords.

00:29:18.290 --> 00:29:21.960
A third finally became law in 1914,

00:29:22.470 --> 00:29:24.080
only to be immediately suspended

00:29:24.220 --> 00:29:26.140
when the First World War broke out.

00:29:26.940 --> 00:29:29.470
So let us take stock.

00:29:29.950 --> 00:29:33.000
If part one was about how England gained

00:29:33.010 --> 00:29:35.190
control of Ireland — five centuries of

00:29:35.220 --> 00:29:37.891
conquest, plantation, and dispossession

00:29:38.330 --> 00:29:40.980
— then this episode has been about what

00:29:40.980 --> 00:29:43.720
happened when Ireland tried to push back.

00:29:44.600 --> 00:29:47.350
Across two centuries, we have seen: a

00:29:47.360 --> 00:29:49.650
failed rebellion that handed Britain

00:29:49.650 --> 00:29:52.170
the justification to abolish Ireland's

00:29:52.210 --> 00:29:54.930
own parliament; a broken promise of

00:29:54.930 --> 00:29:57.730
political rights; a catastrophe that

00:29:57.730 --> 00:30:00.420
killed one million people and sent two

00:30:00.430 --> 00:30:03.450
million more into exile; an underground

00:30:03.460 --> 00:30:05.800
movement that concluded Parliament was

00:30:05.810 --> 00:30:08.390
useless; a brilliant political operator

00:30:08.550 --> 00:30:10.740
who came closer than anyone before him

00:30:10.980 --> 00:30:13.530
to winning Home Rule, brought down not

00:30:13.540 --> 00:30:16.470
by Britain but by a scandal; and a Home

00:30:16.470 --> 00:30:20.090
Rule Act that finally passed in 1914, only

00:30:20.110 --> 00:30:22.460
to be shelved the moment war broke out.

00:30:23.095 --> 00:30:25.180
Ireland was exhausted.

00:30:25.530 --> 00:30:27.250
But it was not finished.

00:30:27.700 --> 00:30:29.620
In the next episode, we find

00:30:29.620 --> 00:30:31.030
out what happens when the

00:30:31.050 --> 00:30:33.130
patience finally runs out.

00:30:34.670 --> 00:30:36.710
OK then, that is it for part

00:30:36.710 --> 00:30:38.420
two of our Ireland series.

00:30:38.680 --> 00:30:40.530
In the third and final episode,

00:30:40.700 --> 00:30:43.100
we follow Ireland to independence:

00:30:43.340 --> 00:30:44.640
through the Easter Rising of

00:30:44.640 --> 00:30:47.300
1916, the War of Independence,

00:30:47.360 --> 00:30:49.020
and the Civil War that followed.

00:30:49.840 --> 00:30:51.830
You've been listening to English Learning

00:30:51.830 --> 00:30:54.390
for Curious Minds by Leonardo English.

00:30:54.780 --> 00:30:57.800
I'm Alastair Budge, you stay safe, and

00:30:57.800 --> 00:30:59.880
I'll catch you in the next episode.

